{
  "$comment": "France. Read after a household's own rules and only filling what they left empty, so nothing here can overrule what somebody wrote about their own statements. Ordered in three bands: 10 to 29 separates a name that two bands would otherwise both claim, 50 to 79 names a shop and files it, 100 and above is the fallback on the words a statement uses whatever the shop. Every rule stops on a match, so the first one that fits decides and the order above is the whole behaviour. Patterns run against the normalised label - upper case, no accents, dates and long digit runs collapsed - and are anchored on word boundaries, because a token like TOTAL or FREE inside another word is not the company.",
  "country": "FR",
  "rules": [
    {
      "$comment": "Both sell fuel and one also sells electricity, so the energy contract is separated before the forecourt rule below can take it.",
      "id": "totalenergies",
      "name": "TotalEnergies",
      "priority": 10,
      "when": {
        "descriptionMatches": "\\bTOTAL\\s?ENERGIES\\b"
      },
      "then": {
        "category": "energy",
        "counterparty": "TotalEnergies"
      },
      "stopOnMatch": true
    },
    {
      "$comment": "A meal, not a ride. Taken before the Uber rule that files rides under transport.",
      "id": "uber-eats",
      "name": "Uber Eats",
      "priority": 11,
      "when": {
        "descriptionMatches": "\\bUBER\\s?EATS\\b"
      },
      "then": {
        "category": "restaurants",
        "counterparty": "Uber Eats"
      },
      "stopOnMatch": true
    },
    {
      "$comment": "A subscription, not a parcel. Taken before the Amazon rule that files parcels under shopping.",
